# Nelder–Mead

Nelder–Mead minimizes a scalar objective using a simplex of `n + 1` points in
`n` dimensions. It compares objective values and moves the simplex through
reflection, expansion, contraction, and shrink steps. You supply no gradient
or Hessian.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/nelder_mead/struct.NelderMead.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` |
| Search | Local, from an initial simplex or one starting point |
| Constraints | Unconstrained by default; an explicit projected variant supports box bounds |
| Parameters | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Consider Nelder–Mead for a small problem when objective values are available
but reliable derivatives are difficult to obtain. Its geometric steps avoid
building a derivative or interpolation model. The original method is due to
[Nelder and Mead (1965)](#ref-nelder-mead-1965).

The simplex stores `n + 1` parameter vectors, so storage grows as `O(n²)`.
Initialization evaluates all of those vertices, and a shrink evaluates `n`
new points. For a smooth objective with expensive evaluations, compare the
quadratic models in [NEWUOA](../newuoa/) or, with box bounds,
[BOBYQA](../bobyqa/). [Solis–Wets](../solis-wets/) uses linear storage and
random trial directions instead.

A collapsed simplex does not certify a stationary point. Nelder–Mead can
converge to a nonminimizer even on some strictly convex two-dimensional
functions; [Lagarias et al. (1998)](#ref-lagarias-et-al-1998) discuss this
limitation and the method's convergence results in low dimensions. Compare
several starting points when the solution matters.

## How it works

Basin sorts the vertices by cost and computes the centroid of every vertex
except the worst. It first reflects the worst vertex through that centroid.
A particularly good reflection prompts an expansion. A poor reflection
prompts a contraction, and a failed contraction shrinks the other vertices
toward the best. Each trial is evaluated before the solver decides which
vertices to retain.

`NelderMead::new()` uses reflection `1`, expansion `2`, contraction `0.5`, and
shrink `0.5`. `NelderMead::adaptive()` selects the dimension-dependent
coefficients of [Gao and Han (2012)](#ref-gao-han-2012); in two dimensions they
coincide with the standard coefficients. `with_params` supplies an explicit
set of coefficients.

The default stopping rule requires both a small simplex in parameter space
and a small spread of its costs. Basin measures the maximum infinity-norm
distance from the best vertex and the maximum absolute cost difference from
that vertex. An unchanged best vertex alone does not stop the search.

## Example

This quadratic has its minimum at `[1.0, -2.0]`. The example uses absolute
simplex tolerances and disables the relative terms so the requested scales
are explicit. The executor supplies an iteration budget.

<!-- compile: solver-nelder-mead -->
```rust
use basin::{CostFunction, Executor, NelderMead, Termination};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + 4.0 * (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = NelderMead::new()
        .with_absolute_simplex_size_tolerance(1e-9)
        .with_relative_simplex_size_tolerance(None)
        .with_absolute_simplex_cost_tolerance(1e-14)
        .with_relative_simplex_cost_tolerance(None);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Quadratic, solver, vec![-3.0, 5.0])
        .max_iter(2_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    assert!((result.param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-7);
    assert!((result.param()[1] + 2.0).abs() < 1e-7);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Choose a useful initial simplex

`Executor::from_start` builds a simplex by changing one coordinate at a time.
Each nonzero coordinate receives a 5% relative perturbation; a zero
coordinate receives an absolute perturbation of `0.00025`. Those defaults
can be much too small or large for a problem's units. Use
`SimplexProgress::with_step` to change the relative perturbation, or pass
`SimplexProgress::from_simplex(vertices)` to `Executor::new` for full control.
Supply `n + 1` vertices of dimension `n` and choose a simplex with nonzero
volume. Rescale coordinates with very different units.

### Interpret the simplex tests

Both absolute tolerances default to `1e-8`. Both relative tolerances default
to `sqrt(f64::EPSILON)` for `f64` or `32 * f32::EPSILON` for `f32`. The size
threshold is the larger of its absolute term and its relative term times
the best point's infinity norm. The cost threshold uses the larger of the
best cost's magnitude and the initial cost spread as its relative scale.

`None` disables an individual tolerance, and zero requests an exact-zero
threshold. The enabled size and cost conditions must both pass. The optional
`with_absolute_simplex_cost_std_dev_tolerance` adds a separate stopping test
based only on the sample standard deviation of the costs. A flat objective
can satisfy cost-only checks far from a useful solution; even the paired
tests describe simplex collapse rather than stationarity. Keep an iteration
or evaluation budget and inspect the termination report.

### Opt into box projection

`NelderMead::new().projected()` requires a problem that implements
`Constraints` with the box-only set `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<Vec<f64>>>`
for a `Vec<f64>` objective. It clamps the initial vertices and every trial
into the declared box before evaluating them. The unbounded constructor
ignores constraint declarations.

Projection can collapse multiple vertices onto the same boundary face and
stall the search. Consider BOBYQA for a box-constrained smooth objective, or
[MADS](../mads/) when a polling method fits the problem. The projected
variant supports box bounds only; it does not handle linear or nonlinear
constraint blocks.

## Implementation

`SimplexProgress` owns the vertices, costs, evaluation counts, and historical
incumbent. The solver owns its scratch vectors and resolved coefficients.
Fresh initialization evaluates every supplied vertex and resets the simplex
convergence reference; an exact solver-and-state checkpoint preserves the
adaptive coefficients, scratch, and reference and skips initialization.

Use `vertices()`, `costs()`, or `evaluated_vertices()` to inspect the simplex.
Opt-in `with_trial_diagnostics(true)` records native trial attempts; retain
the solver through `Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect
`trial_observations()`.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The method
uses vector operations and coordinate access without a matrix factorization
or BLAS/LAPACK provider. The projected mode additionally requires clamping.
See the API's [Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/nelder_mead/struct.NelderMead.html#backends)
for custom-vector requirements.

